# Blog Content Rules

Use `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `.agents/skills/blog-content-strategy/SKILL.md`
as the source of truth for blog work.

When starting a new post, article refresh, or public content brief, apply the
repo-local `technical_writer` sub-agent lens before drafting body copy. Pair it
with `blog-content-strategy` for metadata/references/CTAs and `document-writer`
for xhverse voice. Use the writer lens again before publish to catch weak
claims, missing sources, generic titles, decorative CTAs, or vendor-announcement
tone.

Supabase `posts` is the editorial source of truth. For normal "make/write a blog
post" requests, produce a Supabase Blog Writer-ready article package; do not edit
`src/data/blog.ts` unless the user explicitly asks for a code fallback/seed
change.

After content and cover approval, Codex should complete the publish path when
Supabase and Cloudflare access are available: upload or register the cover,
insert/update the `posts` row, trigger the Pages rebuild, and verify production
`/blog` plus `/blog/<slug>`. Keep `/admin/blog` unchanged as the manual UI and
fallback path when live credentials are unavailable.

## Content Shape

- Blog posts must stay practical, concise, and credible for data/platform peers.
- Before drafting body copy, define:
  - document type (`explanation`, `how-to`, `reference`, `lab companion`)
  - target audience
  - intended reader goal
  - scope boundaries
  - brief outline
- Add complete metadata: slug, title, excerpt, date, tags, reading time, SEO
  title/description, and cover alt text when a cover is present.
- Shape the article for scanning:
  - start with the decision or takeaway
  - use typed callouts only when they clarify the reading path
  - use markdown tables for comparisons, constraints, and decision matrices
  - use normal markdown lists for checklists and decision details; article
    styles render symbolic markers
  - keep paragraphs short enough for mobile reading
- Add `## References` with public URLs for technical claims, design patterns,
  and vendor-specific guidance. Prefer primary docs, vendor docs, standards, or
  widely recognized public references.
- Add `## Disclosure` when AI assists drafting or editing. State that the post
  was co-written with an AI agent and reviewed by Rujikorn Ngoensaard.
- Preserve identity signals where relevant: `Rujikorn Ngoensaard`, `bossruji`,
  `XH`, and `xhverse`.
- Do not invent private client claims or unverifiable production stories.

## Related Tool CTAs

- Prefer typed `relatedTools` metadata over hardcoded one-off CTA sections.
- In the Supabase Blog Writer, enter related tools as `tool-slug:primary` or
  `tool-slug:secondary` lines.
- Use only known internal tool slugs from `/tools`.
- Current high-fit additions: `data-product-contract-builder`, `access-model-simulator`, `lakehouse-table-layout-advisor`, `power-bi-semantic-model-doctor`, and `pipeline-recovery-planner`.
- Keep CTAs lightweight: normal links or static cards, no embedded tool apps.
- Use one primary CTA and no more than two supporting CTAs unless there is a
  clear reader workflow.
- Do not add inline handlers, scripts, dynamic injection, third-party tracking,
  or lead capture to blog CTAs.

## Cover Images

- Store project-bound covers under `public/images/` with stable filenames.
- Use generated covers only when the topic benefits from a real visual asset.
- Match the established xhverse blog cover series by default: dark isometric
  data-architecture illustration, charcoal background, glassmorphism panels,
  teal/cyan glow, restrained amber accents, abstract platform objects, and a
  clear thesis metaphor.
- Avoid readable text inside generated images; diagrams should be recognizable
  by shape, not labels.
- Avoid people, photorealistic office/factory scenes, logos, screenshots,
  generic dashboards, server racks, AI robots, and decorative gradient blobs.
- Keep 16:9 covers thumbnail-readable and resize/crop to the existing 960x540
  pattern when saving static assets.
- Every cover must have useful alt text.

## Verification

- If touching `src/data/blog.ts` or related types, update `src/data/blog.test.ts`
  and run coverage. Normal Supabase-ready content drafts do not require code
  tests because they should not edit the repo.
- For Codex-published Supabase posts, read back the `posts` row, confirm the
  cover URL/path resolves, confirm the rebuild/deployment result, and verify the
  live article route after deployment.
- If touching blog pages, run typecheck and at least the blog route E2E coverage
  if available.
- Full sign-off still requires `bun run check`.
